For the note beside future reports, call it a **reader discrepancy**, not an established artefact. Eight selected cases failed in earlier full-form Codex readings and held in a fresh D-only Codex reading under the unchanged brief; that suggests a context or reader effect but does not identify its cause or show that all 128 earlier marks would change. The two countings bracket the unresolved rulings; they do not determine which reading is correct. The result page’s stronger sentence that the brief “is not what splits” goes beyond that one draw. [Diagnostic scores](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/fresh-a2/d-diagnostic/score-output.txt>)

The census is the right next step. Read the **full question, topic, article and choice as shown**, not only sentences containing a deadline: “will not adopt by the due day” can describe a formal decision against a time-bounded question or a postponement, depending on what was decided. The two third readers agreed that the fresh example catches waiting; keep that ruling while examining the broader class. The old screen explicitly looks for a different pattern, so validate the new screen with known positives, negatives and a sample it did not pick. [Fresh result](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/unlisted-parts/RESULT-FRESH-A2.md>), [existing screen](</Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/stage_a/wait_as_ruling_out_census.py>)

Report both **answers and distinct topics**, because three attempts on one topic are not three independent defects. A count below three is a reason to weigh impact and coverage, not by itself to park a root-caused defect: Alex’s line allows no such error. Decide after the census whether a general repair is supported and what it would affect.

CONSENSUS: no, because the D finding should not yet be called a proven artefact, and the proposed three-answer cutoff would stop work without establishing the class’s impact or mechanism.
